WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:05.150
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:11.750
&gt;&gt; If you could be any superhero,

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.790
what super power would you wanna have?

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:18.990
I bet you are thinking
about it aren't you?

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.040
Well growing up, believe it or not,
this was something my friends and

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.945
I would think about a lot.

00:00:24.945 --> 00:00:29.500
We'd get really excited and we'd say
things like, I wanna fly like superman, or

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.090
I wanna be able to read
10,000 words a minute

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.290
as our parents started to listen in.

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.472
My answer though was always consistent.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:42.690
I wanna make people laugh.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.310
At this point you might be wondering,
how is that a superpower?

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:51.270
I would argue that it is.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.560
Growing up,
my father was a professional comedian, and

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:02.550
I was always fascinated, because he could
get away with pretty much anything.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.350
You can ask my Mom.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.880
I wanted to be just like him.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.110
As it turns out, I'm in business school,
so that didn't quite work out.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.070
But I learned an even bigger lesson,
that humor can actually teach us serious

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.810
lessons about how we can
better relate with each other.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.680
Lessons I didn't quite set out to learn.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.870
In fact, my dad didn't set
out to be a comedian either.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:34.560
20 years ago he moved back from
the United States to his home country,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.370
Ghana, after studying acting
in the United States.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.210
It was a pivotal time in Ghana's history.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.850
For the first time,

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.900
we had democratic elections after two
decades of a military dictatorship.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.790
It was a time of a lot of excitement.

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.500
A lot of hope, but yet

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:59.500
that necessary dose of skepticism that
newly democratized countries tend to have.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.450
And my father could be pretty stubborn
when he was convinced of an idea.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.910
And he was convinced
that the only way this

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.380
democracy would work is if we talked
openly about what was not working.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.700
So to make his point, he goes onto radio.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.060
And of course, the first thing he does,
is publicly criticize the government.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.100
As you can imagine,
that didn't go down too well.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.760
Public reaction to him was intense.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:34.170
His car was vandalized, people wrote
him some pretty creative hate mail.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.780
And the sponsors actually
pulled out from the show

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.030
labeling him as overly controversial.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.810
Fast forward a couple years later.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.620
He returns to his first love,
which is acting.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:51.680
So, he performs in his first one man play,
originally set out to be a drama.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.530
But the audience actually
ended up laughing so

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:57.785
much that it was written about as one of
Ghana's best comedy plays of all time.

00:02:57.785 --> 00:03:01.230
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that is how he became a comedian.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.240
Why am I telling you this story?

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.340
It's because in that play

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.510
he spoke about the exact same issues
he used to talk about on radio.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:18.196
This time though,
people drive more than five hours,

00:03:18.196 --> 00:03:22.520
line up, and pay just to listen to him.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.242
That, ladies and gentleman,
is the power of humor.

00:03:27.242 --> 00:03:34.060
That is why I think it's a superpower, and
that is why I wanted to be just like him.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.910
This fascination with the power of humor
stayed with me through the years and

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.550
I actually ended up
working in the industry.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.260
I worked as a producer for a comedy show.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.680
I learned some pretty hard
lessons very quickly.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.818
The most important being, I'm not funny.

00:03:51.818 --> 00:03:56.530
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In fact, when I told my

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.150
brother that I wanted to make people laugh
for a living, he was quiet for a minute,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.590
looks at me, and says,
I've laughed a lot because of you.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.405
Does that count?

00:04:07.405 --> 00:04:10.738
[Laughter.]
&gt;&gt; So I took a different approach.

00:04:10.738 --> 00:04:14.060
And I sought to understand
what it was about

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.600
humor that enables us to let our
guard down and connect so instantly?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:21.850
And what I found through my research and

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:26.570
my work, is that the magic of
humor is that it's unexpected.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.100
In fact,
our brain works with pattern recognition.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:38.060
So, basically, when you hear a story, you
subconsciously try to auto-complete it.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.890
But when the story goes
in a different direction,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:44.780
we experience this rush
from the surprise factor.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.340
Humor works because we delight

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.170
in the unexpected when it's
coming from a good place.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.270
Research shows that our brains
pleasure center actually

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.090
is far more reactive to experiences
that are both pleasurable and

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.060
unexpected than experiences
that are simply pleasurable.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:11.485
It's the reason why surprise wedding
proposals work so well, most of the time.

00:05:11.485 --> 00:05:15.530
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But how does this connect us?

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.760
Well think back to
the last time you laughed.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:20.990
Hopefully that was a few minutes ago.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.770
Think about how you felt towards
the person that made you laugh.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.320
When we experience this rush,
our brain captures and

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.140
holds that emotional memory, so
the next time we meet again,

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.770
we associate this positive memory with
the person and warm up more quickly.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.430
In other words, if you make a guy or

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.250
girl laugh on the first date,
you're probably gonna get a second one.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.970
So, we know how humor connects and
affects us, but

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.800
not all of us can be professional
comedians, try as we might.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.250
And as I've learned to ask
myself in business school,

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:07.380
how do we apply this framework of using
unexpectedness to delight each other?

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:11.540
Better yet, how do we scale its
affect in our everyday life?

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.720
And I'll tell you a quick story that
helped put it all together for me.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.150
A couple years ago, I lost a bet
to one of my good friends, Sarah.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.740
And like all our bets,
I had to buy her lunch.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:29.460
And so I'm standing in line, I've put
in my order, I'm ready to pay, and

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.390
to my embarrassment,
I've left my wallet again.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.570
And then the weirdest thing happened.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:40.250
A guy walks from behind me, swipes his
credit card, pays for our entire meal and

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.630
walks away before I can
even say thank you.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:53.540
And I'm standing there completely
surprised, but also delighted.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:58.350
And over the years,
with similar experiences and

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:04.480
as I've tried to pay it forward,
I've come to realize that yes,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:11.270
we can delight each other in small,
unexpected acts of kindness.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:17.200
Think back to the last time that
you received words of encouragement

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.920
from a friend you hadn't heard from in
a while, or a friend you just made.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:28.180
Or a time a complete stranger let
you have that first parking spot.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.610
That may be a little too much to ask in
the city but hopefully you get my point.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:38.320
We can all tap into what makes
humor a super power by going

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.240
a little out of our way each day for
the people around us.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:49.430
Even if it's as simple as turning
to the person next to you and

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.410
saying something thoughtful.

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:56.740
You look nice today, even.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:00.220
In this world,
we're often encouraged to think big.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.860
To think of impact in terms of touching
the next million or billion lives.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:08.470
Yet, in our own personal lives,

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:13.280
we think we're touched by individual
moments, and individual people.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:20.290
So, my challenge for you today is to
think small, but make it unexpected.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.468
You'll be pleasantly surprised.

00:08:22.468 --> 00:08:23.870
Thank you.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.889
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:31.649
[MUSIC]

